{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Mozych2b7iAHjqsUpwGtZS","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Implementation rationale — sos_4 \"Together as One\"\n2\t\n3\tCard: **Together as One** — `{6}` Sorcery (Converge).\n4\t\n5\t> Converge — Target player draws X cards, Together as One deals X damage to\n6\t> any target, and you gain X life, where X is the number of colors of mana\n7\t> spent to cast this spell.\n8\t\n9\t## What I implemented\n10\t\n11\t`cards/sos/sos_4/card_impl.py` — `TogetherAsOne(Sorcery)`:\n12\t\n13\t- **Static data** via `kwargs.setdefault` (matches the FDN convention): name\n14\t  `\"Together as One\"`, `mana_cost = ManaCost.parse(\"{6}\")`, rules text. The\n15\t  `Sorcery` base supplies `CardType.SORCERY` and no P/T, satisfying the\n16\t  properties tests.\n17\t- **`colors_spent` default `[]`** in `__init__`. The live cast pipeline\n18\t  (`engine/casting.py`) overwrites this with\n19\t  `list(player.mana_pool.last_payment_colors)` — a de-duplicated,\n20\t  colorless-excluded list of `engine.types.Color`. Defaulting to a list (not\n21\t  `0` like FDN 205) matches the engine-produced format the tests assert and\n22\t  keeps `X == len(self.colors_spent)` well-typed for the X == 0 no-op case.\n23\t- **`get_targets`** returns two `TargetRequirement`s in oracle order:\n24\t  1. *target player* — `filter_fn = _is_player` (accepts only objects with a\n25\t     `life` attribute; a creature has no `life`, so it is rejected — exactly\n26\t     what `test_player_target_rejects_a_creature` checks).\n27\t  2. *any target* — `filter_fn` accepts players or creatures (`_is_player(obj)\n28\t     or _is_creature(obj)`), mirroring FDN 192 (Burst Lightning).\n29\t- **`on_resolve`** computes `X = len(self.colors_spent)` and, only when\n30\t  `X > 0`, applies all three clauses against `self.chosen_targets =\n31\t  [target_player, any_target]`:\n32\t  1. target player draws X cards (`engine.game.draw_card` X times),\n33\t  2. X damage to the any-target (`engine.game.deal_damage` — handles\n34\t     player life loss, creature `damage_marked`, lethal → SBA via the marked\n35\t     damage; protection handled inside `deal_damage`),\n36\t  3. the **controller** (\"you\") gains X life — explicitly `self.controller`,\n37\t     not the target player, so the lifegain-isolation tests pass.\n38\t  `X == 0` returns early, a no-op for every clause.\n39\t\n40\t## Player detection\n41\t\n42\tThe only object in the engine with a `life` attribute is `engine.player.Player`,\n43\tso `hasattr(obj, \"life\")` is the established idiom (used across many FDN cards,\n44\te.g. fdn_192, fdn_91) to distinguish a player target from a creature. I used a\n45\tsmall module-level `_is_player` / `_is_creature` helper for readability.\n46\t\n47\t## Engine changes\n48\t\n49\t**None.** Converge / `colors_spent` is already fully wired:\n50\t`engine/casting.cast_spell` assigns `card.colors_spent =\n51\tlist(player.mana_pool.last_payment_colors)` after payment, and\n52\t`engine/mana.MannaPool.last_payment_colors` already returns the distinct,\n53\tcolorless-excluded, sorted color list. The end-to-end `TestTogetherAsOneFullCast`\n54\ttest confirms the card reads the engine-populated value (3 colors → X == 3).\n55\t\n56\t## Spec deviations\n57\t\n58\tNone. The implementation follows the oracle text exactly. The only judgment call\n59\t(noted by the tester and the engine) is that optimizing the generic-mana split to\n60\tmaximize distinct colors is deferred in the engine (`# TODO: Phase 3` in\n61\t`engine/casting.py`); the card simply consumes whatever colors the engine reports.\n62\t\n63\t## Verification\n64\t\n65\t- `pytest cards/sos/sos_4/tests.py` → 20 passed.\n66\t- Full `pytest` from `/workspace` → 1230 passed (no regressions).\n67\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SBz24qcJ2ns1fWGdmmmuWu","session_id":"69c150b5-c49a-4f71-a445-032aa09f64bc","uuid":"292b1678-64df-49fb-9245-e2645490109f","timestamp":"2026-06-02T07:53:23.114Z","subagent_type":"Reviewer","task_description":"Review sos_4 implementation"}
