{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D6hLvcBe9XBegvbspbczfD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Test rationale — sos_13 (Emeritus of Truce // Swords to Plowshares)\n2\t\n3\t## Card under test\n4\t\n5\tFront (creature) face: **Emeritus of Truce** — `{1}{W}{W}` — Creature — Cat Cleric — 3/3 — white — mythic.\n6\t\n7\tOracle (front face):\n8\t\n9\t> When this creature enters, target player creates a 1/1 white and black\n10\t> Inkling creature token with flying. Then if an opponent controls more\n11\t> creatures than you, this creature becomes prepared. (While it's prepared,\n12\t> you may cast a copy of its spell. Doing so unprepares it.)\n13\t\n14\tBack (prepare) half: **Swords to Plowshares** — `{W}` — Instant.\n15\t\n16\tThis is a **Preparation card** (Comprehensive Rules 722): the front face is a\n17\tpermanent that can \"become prepared\", and when it does, its controller creates\n18\ta copy of the prepare-spell half (the `{W}` instant) in exile that they may\n19\tcast; casting it unprepares the permanent (722.3a–c). The \"Prepared\" mechanic\n20\tand split/preparation card framing have **no existing engine support** — the\n21\tengine has no `Keyword.PREPARED`, no split/DFC card model, and no\n22\tcopy-to-exile / cast-from-exile plumbing for prepare spells. The tests\n23\ttherefore concentrate on the clearly specified, behaviourally observable\n24\tcontract and probe the prepared *designation* in an implementation-agnostic\n25\tway (see `untestable.json` for the parts deferred).\n26\t\n27\t## Behaviour contract derived from the spec\n28\t\n29\t1. Front face is a white Creature — Cat Cleric, 3/3, mana cost `{1}{W}{W}`.\n30\t2. ETB triggered ability (watches `EntersBattlefieldTriggeredEvent`, fires only\n31\t   for this card): a **target player** (chosen by the controller — may be the\n32\t   controller or an opponent) creates **one** 1/1 white-and-black **Inkling**\n33\t   creature token with flying, under the *targeted* player's control.\n34\t3. After the token resolves, the creature **becomes prepared** **iff** an\n35\t   opponent controls **strictly more** creatures than the controller. Equal or\n36\t   fewer ⇒ not prepared.\n37\t4. The card carries a back/prepare half — Swords to Plowshares, an Instant\n38\t   costing `{W}`.\n39\t\n40\t## Test file\n41\t\n42\t`cards/sos/sos_13/tests.py` — 21 test cases across five classes.\n43\t\n44\t### TestEmeritusProperties (front-face static data) — 6 tests\n45\t- `test_is_creature` — front face is a `Creature` with `CardType.CREATURE`.\n46\t- `test_name_includes_emeritus_of_truce` — name contains \"Emeritus of Truce\".\n47\t  Lenient substring match (rejects wrong names) because a split/preparation\n48\t  card may legitimately expose either the front-half name or the combined\n49\t  split name; the exact spelling is an implementation-style choice, not a spec\n50\t  requirement.\n51\t- `test_front_face_mana_cost` — castable half costs `{1}{W}{W}`.\n52\t- `test_power_toughness` — base P/T 3/3.\n53\t- `test_is_white` — colour set is `{WHITE}` via `engine.protection.get_colors`\n54\t  (works whether colour is from an explicit `colors` attribute or the mana\n55\t  cost pips — does not over-constrain representation).\n56\t- `test_subtypes_cat_cleric` — subtypes include \"Cat\" and \"Cleric\".\n57\t\n58\t### TestEmeritusEtbTriggerRegistration — 4 tests\n59\tMirrors the FDN ETB idiom (fdn_84 Dragon Trainer / fdn_136 Angel of Finality):\n60\tthe card registers a self-referencing ETB trigger via `register_triggers`.\n61\t- `test_registers_one_trigger` — exactly one trigger is added.\n62\t- `test_trigger_watches_enters_battlefield_event` — the registration is a\n63\t  `TriggerRegistration` whose `event_type is EntersBattlefieldTriggeredEvent`.\n64\t- `test_trigger_only_fires_for_this_card` — firing an ETB event for a\n65\t  *different* permanent does not push this card's trigger (guards against a\n66\t  too-broad condition). This is a negative assertion; it currently passes\n67\t  against the empty stub but remains a meaningful guard once implemented and is\n68\t  paired with the two positive registration tests above.\n69\t- `test_trigger_fires_when_this_card_enters` — firing the ETB event for this\n70\t  card pushes a stack object whose `source` is the card.\n71\t\n72\tNote on engine timing: `engine.zones.move_to_zone` fires\n73\t`EntersBattlefieldTriggeredEvent` *before* calling `register_triggers`, so a\n74\tself-ETB trigger never self-fires on the card's own entry through the normal\n75\tpipeline. Following the proven sos_1 / FDN convention, these tests register\n76\ttriggers and then **explicitly fire** the ETB event to exercise the effect.\n77\t\n78\t### TestEmeritusTokenCreation — 6 tests\n79\tVerifies the token half of the ETB.\n80\t- `test_controller_target_gets_one_inkling` — targeting the controller yields\n81\t  exactly one new Inkling token on their battlefield.\n82\t- `test_token_is_one_one` — token base P/T is 1/1.\n83\t- `test_token_has_flying` — token has `Keyword.FLYING`.\n84\t- `test_token_is_white_and_black` — token colours are `{WHITE, BLACK}` via\n85\t  `get_colors` (representation-agnostic).\n86\t- `test_targeting_opponent_creates_token_under_opponent` — \"target player\" can\n87\t  be an opponent; the token then enters under the *opponent's* control and the\n88\t  controller gets none. (Edge case: target is not always \"you\".)\n89\t- `test_token_is_a_creature_token` — token has `CardType.CREATURE` and\n90\t  `is_token is True`.\n91\t\n92\tToken detection is tolerant: any battlefield `Creature` flagged `is_token`\n93\twhose name or subtypes mention \"Inkling\". This avoids coupling to an exact\n94\tnaming scheme while still asserting the meaningful characteristics.\n95\t\n96\t### TestEmeritusBecomesPrepared — 4 tests\n97\tVerifies the conditional \"becomes prepared\" clause. Every board state is\n98\tdeliberately constructed so the prepared/not-prepared outcome is **the same\n99\twhether the implementation compares creature counts before or after the Inkling\n100\ttoken is created** — by directing the token to the player whose count cannot\n101\tcross the strict-greater-than threshold. This removes a real ambiguity (the\n102\toracle says \"create a token. Then if ...\", i.e. sequential within one\n103\tresolution) from the contract so the test does not punish a reasonable\n104\timplementation choice.\n105\t- `test_becomes_prepared_when_opponent_has_more_creatures` — opponent 3, you 1,\n106\t  token sent to opponent ⇒ opponent strictly more ⇒ prepared. (Also asserts the\n107\t  token was created, anchoring the assertion to real ETB execution.)\n108\t- `test_not_prepared_when_counts_equal` — you 2, opponent 2, token to you ⇒\n109\t  opponent never strictly more ⇒ not prepared. \"Strictly more\" boundary.\n110\t- `test_not_prepared_when_controller_has_more_creatures` — you 3, opponent 0,\n111\t  token to opponent ⇒ you ahead ⇒ not prepared.\n112\t- `test_not_prepared_when_opponent_has_no_creatures` — opponent 0, you 1 ⇒ not\n113\t  prepared.\n114\t\n115\tThe three \"not prepared\" tests each **also assert the Inkling token was\n116\tcreated**. Without that anchor they would pass trivially against the empty stub\n117\t(an unimplemented mechanic never sets the prepared flag, so the\n118\t`is_prepared == False` check would be a false green). Anchoring to token\n119\tcreation guarantees the not-prepared branch is only reached after the\n120\timplemented ETB has actually run.\n121\t\n122\tThe prepared *designation* is read through a small `_is_prepared` helper that\n123\tprobes `is_prepared` / `prepared` boolean attributes, so the tests are not\n124\tcoupled to one spelling of the new mechanic.\n125\t\n126\t### TestSwordsToPlowsharesHalf — 1 test\n127\t- `test_prepare_spell_mana_cost_is_w` — the prepare (\"Swords to Plowshares\")\n128\t  half costs `{W}`. The implementation may expose the prepare spell's cost via\n129\t  a dedicated attribute (`prepare_mana_cost`, `back_mana_cost`, …) or via a\n130\t  back-face object/factory; the test probes the common shapes and asserts at\n131\t  least one yields `ManaCost.parse(\"{W}\")`. This is the one spec fact about the\n132\t  back half that is unambiguously testable without an engine preparation-card\n133\t  framework.\n134\t\n135\t## Edge cases covered\n136\t- \"target player\" pointed at an opponent (token under opponent's control).\n137\t- \"Strictly more\" boundary (equal counts ⇒ not prepared).\n138\t- Controller ahead and opponent empty (⇒ not prepared).\n139\t- ETB trigger must not fire for an unrelated permanent's entry.\n140\t\n141\t## Edge cases intentionally NOT covered (see untestable.json)\n142\t- The copy-of-the-prepare-spell-in-exile creation on becoming prepared\n143\t  (722.3c), casting that copy, and the unprepare-on-cast transition: no engine\n144\t  support exists for preparation-card copies or casting them from exile.\n145\t- The actual **Swords to Plowshares effect** (\"Exile target creature; its\n146\t  controller gains life equal to its power\") — the spec's `oracle_text` does\n147\t  not include the back-face rules text, so the back half's resolution behaviour\n148\t  is not specified here and cannot be asserted from this card's spec.\n149\t- Casting timing / sorcery-vs-instant restrictions on the prepare copy.\n150\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"69c150b5-c49a-4f71-a445-032aa09f64bc","uuid":"0d95448f-1666-4bd9-8bd1-2658cdfce3a5","timestamp":"2026-06-02T08:02:10.985Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/coordinator-run/sos_13/test-rationale.md","content":"# Test rationale — sos_13 (Emeritus of Truce // Swords to Plowshares)\n\n## Card under test\n\nFront (creature) face: **Emeritus of Truce** — `{1}{W}{W}` — Creature — Cat Cleric — 3/3 — white — mythic.\n\nOracle (front face):\n\n> When this creature enters, target player creates a 1/1 white and black\n> Inkling creature token with flying. Then if an opponent controls more\n> creatures than you, this creature becomes prepared. (While it's prepared,\n> you may cast a copy of its spell. Doing so unprepares it.)\n\nBack (prepare) half: **Swords to Plowshares** — `{W}` — Instant.\n\nThis is a **Preparation card** (Comprehensive Rules 722): the front face is a\npermanent that can \"become prepared\", and when it does, its controller creates\na copy of the prepare-spell half (the `{W}` instant) in exile that they may\ncast; casting it unprepares the permanent (722.3a–c). The \"Prepared\" mechanic\nand split/preparation card framing have **no existing engine support** — the\nengine has no `Keyword.PREPARED`, no split/DFC card model, and no\ncopy-to-exile / cast-from-exile plumbing for prepare spells. The tests\ntherefore concentrate on the clearly specified, behaviourally observable\ncontract and probe the prepared *designation* in an implementation-agnostic\nway (see `untestable.json` for the parts deferred).\n\n## Behaviour contract derived from the spec\n\n1. Front face is a white Creature — Cat Cleric, 3/3, mana cost `{1}{W}{W}`.\n2. ETB triggered ability (watches `EntersBattlefieldTriggeredEvent`, fires only\n   for this card): a **target player** (chosen by the controller — may be the\n   controller or an opponent) creates **one** 1/1 white-and-black **Inkling**\n   creature token with flying, under the *targeted* player's control.\n3. After the token resolves, the creature **becomes prepared** **iff** an\n   opponent controls **strictly more** creatures than the controller. Equal or\n   fewer ⇒ not prepared.\n4. The card carries a back/prepare half — Swords to Plowshares, an Instant\n   costing `{W}`.\n\n## Test file\n\n`cards/sos/sos_13/tests.py` — 21 test cases across five classes.\n\n### TestEmeritusProperties (front-face static data) — 6 tests\n- `test_is_creature` — front face is a `Creature` with `CardType.CREATURE`.\n- `test_name_includes_emeritus_of_truce` — name contains \"Emeritus of Truce\".\n  Lenient substring match (rejects wrong names) because a split/preparation\n  card may legitimately expose either the front-half name or the combined\n  split name; the exact spelling is an implementation-style choice, not a spec\n  requirement.\n- `test_front_face_mana_cost` — castable half costs `{1}{W}{W}`.\n- `test_power_toughness` — base P/T 3/3.\n- `test_is_white` — colour set is `{WHITE}` via `engine.protection.get_colors`\n  (works whether colour is from an explicit `colors` attribute or the mana\n  cost pips — does not over-constrain representation).\n- `test_subtypes_cat_cleric` — subtypes include \"Cat\" and \"Cleric\".\n\n### TestEmeritusEtbTriggerRegistration — 4 tests\nMirrors the FDN ETB idiom (fdn_84 Dragon Trainer / fdn_136 Angel of Finality):\nthe card registers a self-referencing ETB trigger via `register_triggers`.\n- `test_registers_one_trigger` — exactly one trigger is added.\n- `test_trigger_watches_enters_battlefield_event` — the registration is a\n  `TriggerRegistration` whose `event_type is EntersBattlefieldTriggeredEvent`.\n- `test_trigger_only_fires_for_this_card` — firing an ETB event for a\n  *different* permanent does not push this card's trigger (guards against a\n  too-broad condition). This is a negative assertion; it currently passes\n  against the empty stub but remains a meaningful guard once implemented and is\n  paired with the two positive registration tests above.\n- `test_trigger_fires_when_this_card_enters` — firing the ETB event for this\n  card pushes a stack object whose `source` is the card.\n\nNote on engine timing: `engine.zones.move_to_zone` fires\n`EntersBattlefieldTriggeredEvent` *before* calling `register_triggers`, so a\nself-ETB trigger never self-fires on the card's own entry through the normal\npipeline. Following the proven sos_1 / FDN convention, these tests register\ntriggers and then **explicitly fire** the ETB event to exercise the effect.\n\n### TestEmeritusTokenCreation — 6 tests\nVerifies the token half of the ETB.\n- `test_controller_target_gets_one_inkling` — targeting the controller yields\n  exactly one new Inkling token on their battlefield.\n- `test_token_is_one_one` — token base P/T is 1/1.\n- `test_token_has_flying` — token has `Keyword.FLYING`.\n- `test_token_is_white_and_black` — token colours are `{WHITE, BLACK}` via\n  `get_colors` (representation-agnostic).\n- `test_targeting_opponent_creates_token_under_opponent` — \"target player\" can\n  be an opponent; the token then enters under the *opponent's* control and the\n  controller gets none. (Edge case: target is not always \"you\".)\n- `test_token_is_a_creature_token` — token has `CardType.CREATURE` and\n  `is_token is True`.\n\nToken detection is tolerant: any battlefield `Creature` flagged `is_token`\nwhose name or subtypes mention \"Inkling\". This avoids coupling to an exact\nnaming scheme while still asserting the meaningful characteristics.\n\n### TestEmeritusBecomesPrepared — 4 tests\nVerifies the conditional \"becomes prepared\" clause. Every board state is\ndeliberately constructed so the prepared/not-prepared outcome is **the same\nwhether the implementation compares creature counts before or after the Inkling\ntoken is created** — by directing the token to the player whose count cannot\ncross the strict-greater-than threshold. This removes a real ambiguity (the\noracle says \"create a token. Then if ...\", i.e. sequential within one\nresolution) from the contract so the test does not punish a reasonable\nimplementation choice.\n- `test_becomes_prepared_when_opponent_has_more_creatures` — opponent 3, you 1,\n  token sent to opponent ⇒ opponent strictly more ⇒ prepared. (Also asserts the\n  token was created, anchoring the assertion to real ETB execution.)\n- `test_not_prepared_when_counts_equal` — you 2, opponent 2, token to you ⇒\n  opponent never strictly more ⇒ not prepared. \"Strictly more\" boundary.\n- `test_not_prepared_when_controller_has_more_creatures` — you 3, opponent 0,\n  token to opponent ⇒ you ahead ⇒ not prepared.\n- `test_not_prepared_when_opponent_has_no_creatures` — opponent 0, you 1 ⇒ not\n  prepared.\n\nThe three \"not prepared\" tests each **also assert the Inkling token was\ncreated**. Without that anchor they would pass trivially against the empty stub\n(an unimplemented mechanic never sets the prepared flag, so the\n`is_prepared == False` check would be a false green). Anchoring to token\ncreation guarantees the not-prepared branch is only reached after the\nimplemented ETB has actually run.\n\nThe prepared *designation* is read through a small `_is_prepared` helper that\nprobes `is_prepared` / `prepared` boolean attributes, so the tests are not\ncoupled to one spelling of the new mechanic.\n\n### TestSwordsToPlowsharesHalf — 1 test\n- `test_prepare_spell_mana_cost_is_w` — the prepare (\"Swords to Plowshares\")\n  half costs `{W}`. The implementation may expose the prepare spell's cost via\n  a dedicated attribute (`prepare_mana_cost`, `back_mana_cost`, …) or via a\n  back-face object/factory; the test probes the common shapes and asserts at\n  least one yields `ManaCost.parse(\"{W}\")`. This is the one spec fact about the\n  back half that is unambiguously testable without an engine preparation-card\n  framework.\n\n## Edge cases covered\n- \"target player\" pointed at an opponent (token under opponent's control).\n- \"Strictly more\" boundary (equal counts ⇒ not prepared).\n- Controller ahead and opponent empty (⇒ not prepared).\n- ETB trigger must not fire for an unrelated permanent's entry.\n\n## Edge cases intentionally NOT covered (see untestable.json)\n- The copy-of-the-prepare-spell-in-exile creation on becoming prepared\n  (722.3c), casting that copy, and the unprepare-on-cast transition: no engine\n  support exists for preparation-card copies or casting them from exile.\n- The actual **Swords to Plowshares effect** (\"Exile target creature; its\n  controller gains life equal to its power\") — the spec's `oracle_text` does\n  not include the back-face rules text, so the back half's resolution behaviour\n  is not specified here and cannot be asserted from this card's spec.\n- Casting timing / sorcery-vs-instant restrictions on the prepare copy.\n","numLines":150,"startLine":1,"totalLines":150}}}
