{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QFUeEmTp1SrhhndMtnQpF7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\"severity\": \"strict\", \"file\": \"engine/preparation.py\", \"line\": 515, \"comment\": \"cast_prepared_copy unprepares the source and pops the registry entry BEFORE calling cast_spell_free, but cast_spell_free can raise CastingError (e.g. no legal target / target has protection) and roll the copy back into exile. On that failure path the permanent has been wrongly unprepared and the surviving exile copy is orphaned (get_prepared_copy now returns None for a copy still sitting in exile). CR 722.3c says the designation is lost only 'at the time the spell becomes cast' — i.e. on success. Cast first, then unprepare/detach only after cast_spell_free returns without error (or wrap in try/except and restore the designation + registry entry on failure).\"},\n3\t  {\"severity\": \"advisory\", \"file\": \"engine/preparation.py\", \"line\": 357, \"comment\": \"The per-game registry keys by id(permanent) and stores only a strong ref to the copy, not to the permanent. If a prepared permanent leaves the battlefield and is garbage-collected without unprepare() being called, its id can later be reused by a different object, and get_prepared_copy/get_prepared_source could return the stale copy (and the copy leaks, holding a strong ref to the now-dead permanent via copy.prepare_source). This mirrors the sanctioned sos_1 id()-keyed registry pattern so it is not a blocker, but consider keying via the permanent's stable object_id (GameObject.object_id) instead of id(permanent) to avoid id-reuse aliasing.\"},\n4\t  {\"severity\": \"advisory\", \"file\": \"engine/preparation.py\", \"line\": 480, \"comment\": \"get_prepared_source's fallback loop is dead code: it is marked '# pragma: no cover', iterates the registry only to unconditionally return None, and the comment concedes id() cannot be reversed. Drop the loop and just return None after the back-link check.\"},\n5\t  {\"severity\": \"advisory\", \"file\": \"engine/preparation.py\", \"line\": 515, \"comment\": \"If get_prepared_source returns None (a copy lacking a prepare_source back-link), cast_prepared_copy silently skips clearing the designation and leaves any matching registry entry in place, yet still casts the copy. Consider also clearing by reverse-scanning the registry for the copy so an externally-supplied copy still unprepares its source.\"},\n6\t  {\"severity\": \"advisory\", \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_13/card_impl.py\", \"line\": 281, \"comment\": \"The script-fallback branch in _get_target_player calls controller.choose_target(list(game.players), 'target player'), passing a list of Players where the engine's choose_target expects (target_specs, spec). It is only reached when chosen_targets is empty (tests always set chosen_targets), but the argument shapes do not match the engine idiom and could misbehave against a real controller script. Prefer building a TargetRequirement (zone-less / player filter) or matching the established choose_target signature.\"},\n7\t  {\"severity\": \"advisory\", \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_13/card_impl.py\", \"line\": 104, \"comment\": \"In SwordsToPlowshares.on_resolve the locals 'owner' and 'from_bf' are computed but unused for the actual move — owner is never passed and the from_bf loop just confirms presence before calling move_to_zone(BATTLEFIELD->EXILE), which already locates the card itself. Dead bookkeeping; can be simplified.\"}\n8\t]\n9\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01RVMC8pWzD1khWv5YamS13i","session_id":"69c150b5-c49a-4f71-a445-032aa09f64bc","uuid":"5ea53c30-7380-4555-bab2-5db549079aa4","timestamp":"2026-06-02T08:13:48.288Z","subagent_type":"Implementer","task_description":"Revise sos_13 per review"}
