{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PS9n1RxkA3tZkGDaFsBqwa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Test rationale — sos_226 \"Silverquill, the Disputant\"\n2\t\n3\t## Behaviour contract under test\n4\t\n5\t`{2}{W}{B}` Legendary Creature — Elder Dragon, 4/4, colors B+W, Flying + Vigilance.\n6\t\n7\tOracle:\n8\t> Flying, vigilance\n9\t> Each instant and sorcery spell you cast has casualty 1. (As you cast that spell,\n10\t> you may sacrifice a creature with power 1 or greater. When you do, copy the spell\n11\t> and you may choose new targets for the copy.)\n12\t\n13\tTwo contracts:\n14\t\n15\t1. **Static card data** — types, cost (mv 4), P/T 4/4, LEGENDARY supertype,\n16\t   {Dragon, Elder} subtypes, Flying + Vigilance evergreen keywords, B+W colors.\n17\t2. **Casualty-granting continuous static ability** — while Silverquill is on the\n18\t   battlefield, every instant and sorcery SPELL its controller casts \"has\n19\t   casualty 1\" (CR 702.153a). Casualty 1 = \"as an additional cost you may\n20\t   sacrifice a creature with power 1 or greater; when you do, copy the spell and\n21\t   may choose new targets for the copy.\"\n22\t\n23\tThe engine has **no casualty pipeline** (confirmed: only sos_226's own spec and\n24\tRULEBOOK.txt mention \"casualty\"). Following the precedent set by sos_201's\n25\tmiracle tests, the casualty *grant* is probed through a tolerant observable\n26\tsurface (a query method on the card and/or an attribute on the affected spell),\n27\tand the casualty *resolution* mechanic (paying the cost by sacrificing a\n28\tpower>=1 creature, then producing a copy with optional new targets) is deferred\n29\tto `untestable.json` until the Coordinator decides whether to build engine\n30\tsupport.\n31\t\n32\t## File: `cards/sos/sos_226/tests.py`\n33\t\n34\t### `TestSilverquillProperties` — static data\n35\t- `test_is_creature` — is a `Creature` subclass.\n36\t- `test_name` — name is exactly \"Silverquill, the Disputant\".\n37\t- `test_mana_cost` — cost parses to `{2}{W}{B}`.\n38\t- `test_mana_value_is_four` — mana value 4.\n39\t- `test_power_toughness` — base P/T is 4/4.\n40\t- `test_is_legendary` — LEGENDARY supertype.\n41\t- `test_is_elder_dragon` — subtypes include both \"Dragon\" and \"Elder\".\n42\t- `test_has_flying` — FLYING evergreen keyword present.\n43\t- `test_has_vigilance` — VIGILANCE evergreen keyword present.\n44\t- `test_is_white_black` — cost pips are exactly one W and one B, zero R/U/G.\n45\t  This is the canonical, surface-independent colour pin (sos_201 used the same\n46\t  pip-based approach).\n47\t- `test_colors_are_black_and_white` — if the impl exposes a `colors` attribute\n48\t  it must be exactly `{WHITE, BLACK}`; skips (the pip test covers colours)\n49\t  otherwise, so it never fails just because the impl chose not to expose a\n50\t  redundant attribute.\n51\t- `test_casualty_is_printed_keyword_label_not_enum` — enforces the frozen-enum\n52\t  convention from KEY_DECISIONS: \"Casualty\" is NOT a `Keyword` enum member\n53\t  (`Keyword` is frozen at 16; `engine_tests/test_types.py` hard-asserts that).\n54\t  Asserts `Keyword` has no `CASUALTY` member; if the card exposes\n55\t  `printed_keywords`, \"Casualty\" must be among the labels (skips that half if no\n56\t  such surface exists).\n57\t\n58\t### `TestSilverquillCasualtyGrant` — the casualty grant surface\n59\tTolerant probe `_casualty_value_for(...)` tries, in order: card query methods\n60\t(`casualty_value_for`/`get_casualty_value`/`casualty_for`/`get_casualty`/\n61\t`casualty_value`) then spell attributes (`casualty_value`/`casualty`/\n62\t`granted_casualty`); `pytest.skip` with a clear reason if none exists (so the\n63\tcontract is never silently green). `_coerce_to_one(...)` normalises the reported\n64\tvalue (int / numeric string / object `.value`/`.n`/`.amount`/`.cmc`) to \"== 1\",\n65\tand deliberately treats a bare `True` flag as NOT a confirmation of the specific\n66\tvalue 1 (a card that merely flags \"has casualty\" without N=1 should not pass).\n67\t\n68\t- `test_instant_gets_casualty_one` — a controller-hand instant is granted\n69\t  casualty 1.\n70\t- `test_sorcery_gets_casualty_one` — a controller-hand sorcery is granted\n71\t  casualty 1 (the grant covers sorceries, not just instants).\n72\t- `test_creature_spell_does_not_get_casualty` — scope: a creature spell is\n73\t  neither instant nor sorcery, so no casualty. **Anchored** on a real instant\n74\t  (via the skipping probe) so an empty stub fails rather than passing vacuously,\n75\t  then asserts the creature is excluded via the silent probe.\n76\t- `test_opponents_instant_does_not_get_casualty` — scope: only spells YOU cast\n77\t  get casualty; an opponent's instant is unaffected. Anchored on a\n78\t  controller-side instant.\n79\t- `test_no_grant_while_off_battlefield` — a permanent's static ability functions\n80\t  only while on the battlefield. With Silverquill in hand the grant is off.\n81\t  Anchored on a parallel on-battlefield setup that DOES grant, so an impl that\n82\t  always returns a value is caught while a genuinely-absent surface skips.\n83\t\n84\t### `TestSilverquillGrantWiring`\n85\t- `test_card_exposes_a_casualty_grant_surface` — the **hard anchor**: the impl\n86\t  must expose at least one recognised casualty-grant surface (a card query\n87\t  method, or an attribute it writes onto an affected spell). This is the one\n88\t  grant-side test that FAILS (not skips) against the empty stub, guaranteeing\n89\t  the casualty contract is never silently green even if every tolerant probe\n90\t  skips.\n91\t\n92\t## Why \"spell\" is modelled as a hand card\n93\t\n94\tThe probe puts the affected instant/sorcery in the controller's hand and queries\n95\tthe grant there. CR 702.153 grants casualty \"as you cast\" the spell; the grant\n96\tis a continuous static ability whose effect modifies how the spell is cast. With\n97\tno casting/casualty pipeline, the controller's-hand instants/sorceries are the\n98\tnatural observable proxy (mirrors exactly how sos_201's miracle grant is probed\n99\ton hand cards). The implementer is free to surface the grant on the actual\n100\tstack-side spell too — the probe checks the spell object regardless of zone.\n101\t\n102\t## Edge cases covered\n103\t- Instants AND sorceries both granted (two separate tests).\n104\t- Creature spells excluded (wrong card type).\n105\t- Opponent's spells excluded (wrong controller).\n106\t- Off-battlefield → no grant (static-ability functioning zone).\n107\t- \"Casualty\" is a printed label, not a smuggled Keyword enum member.\n108\t- A bare `True` \"has-casualty\" flag does not satisfy the N==1 assertions.\n109\t\n110\t## Edge cases deliberately NOT covered here (see untestable.json)\n111\t- **Paying the casualty cost** (optionally sacrificing a power>=1 creature as an\n112\t  additional cost as the spell is cast).\n113\t- **The power threshold** itself (only creatures with power >= 1 are legal\n114\t  sacrifices for casualty 1).\n115\t- **The copy trigger** (\"When you do, copy the spell\") and the **optional new\n116\t  targets** for the copy.\n117\t- **Stacking of casualty** (the spell having casualty from elsewhere too — CR\n118\t  702.153b — out of scope for this single card).\n119\tThese all require an engine casualty pipeline (additional-cost hook + copy +\n120\tretarget) that does not exist; asserting them now would only test a fixture we'd\n121\thave to invent. Recorded in `untestable.json` with concrete unblock notes so the\n122\tCoordinator can decide whether to commission the engine support and re-invoke.\n123\t\n124\t## Red-phase status\n125\tAgainst the empty stub: **11 failed, 7 skipped, 0 errors** (no import errors).\n126\t- The static-data tests and the hard grant-wiring anchor FAIL.\n127\t- The 7 tolerant grant/colour/printed-label probes SKIP-with-reason (no surface\n128\t  yet), so the contract is explicit, not silently green.\n129\tVerified with a throwaway reference implementation that all 18 tests turn green,\n130\tconfirming the tolerant probes recognise a correct implementation.\n131\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015WsNuwKx3ww7UKaCjA1Mn7","session_id":"69c150b5-c49a-4f71-a445-032aa09f64bc","uuid":"d36a3117-367e-48b6-8fa6-2424c768ccc0","timestamp":"2026-06-02T09:31:40.693Z","subagent_type":"Implementer","task_description":"Implement sos_226 + casualty framework"}
